Among Black respondents (race = "black") in this NLSY-1979 file - which contains no income variable, so economic
level is operationalized as prior wealth (composite_wealth_1985) - incarceration (ever_jailed, jails 1985-1994) is
associated with weaker wealth growth across 1985 -> 1990 -> 1996 at every prior-wealth level of Black men, and the
dollar shortfall grows monotonically with prior wealth: about -$18k to -$34k at the low level (w1985 <= 0), -$24k
to -$27k at the middle level ($0-$5,000) and -$44k to -$80k at the high level (> $5,000), a high-minus-low
gradient of roughly -$57k (95% CI -$92k to -$22k), so that by 1996 jailed men held only about 16% of the mean
wealth of comparable non-jailed Black men at the high level versus about 28-30% at the low and middle levels, with
high-level jailed men losing wealth on average (-$6.1k) while their non-jailed peers gained +$70.4k. Matching on
composite_wealth_1985 erases about 73% of the raw pooled gap (-$39.5k unmatched to -$10.8k matched) and only the
high-wealth shortfall remains distinguishable from zero under strict 1:1 matching (-$45k, 95% CI -$77k to -$15k),
so this is an association conditional on starting position - largely, though not entirely, reflecting that jailed
men started lower, with a residual deficit concentrated where the most wealth was at stake - and not an estimate
of what jail does to wealth on its own; in relative (percentile-rank) terms the deficit is similar at all three
levels (about 11-19 percentile points) and the cross-level gradient is not statistically detectable (-0.064, 95%
CI -0.162 to +0.036), so the gradient is one of dollar losses rather than of relative mobility, and the results
for Black women (16 jailed of 1,548) are not conclusive.
